WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.166
A steel paperclip, aluminium
strip, copper wire and wooden

2
00:00:03.166 --> 00:00:04.749
bead sit behind a clear line.

3
00:00:04.749 --> 00:00:06.987
A bar magnet approaches
without touching.

4
00:00:06.987 --> 00:00:08.297
Which objects will move?

5
00:00:08.297 --> 00:00:11.627
Choose before the magnet crosses
the distance markers, and do

6
00:00:11.627 --> 00:00:13.101
not use shiny as your rule.

7
00:00:13.101 --> 00:00:15.333
The steel paperclip
moves across the gap.

8
00:00:15.333 --> 00:00:18.165
In this test, the aluminium,
copper and wood do not.

9
00:00:18.165 --> 00:00:21.323
Magnetic force can act without
contact, but magnets do not

10
00:00:21.323 --> 00:00:22.412
attract every metal.

11
00:00:22.412 --> 00:00:25.243
Material evidence matters
more than colour or shine.

12
00:00:25.243 --> 00:00:28.332
Reset each object at the same
two-centimetre gap and repeat

13
00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:28.960
three times.

14
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.207
Steel responds each time; the
others show no visible movement.

15
00:00:32.207 --> 00:00:35.191
The repeated pattern supports
a careful claim about these

16
00:00:35.191 --> 00:00:37.704
tested materials, not
every object in the world.

17
00:00:51.973 --> 00:00:56.002
A bar magnet has two poles,
marked north in red and south in

18
00:00:56.002 --> 00:00:56.338
blue.

19
00:00:56.338 --> 00:00:58.755
Keep those colours
and labels fixed.

20
00:00:58.755 --> 00:01:02.919
Bring a north pole near a south
pole, leaving a visible gap so

21
00:01:02.919 --> 00:01:05.806
the non-contact
interaction is easy to see.

22
00:01:05.806 --> 00:01:06.224
Release.

23
00:01:06.224 --> 00:01:07.581
The magnets move together.

24
00:01:07.581 --> 00:01:10.765
Attraction means the magnetic
forces pull the magnets towards

25
00:01:10.765 --> 00:01:11.340
each other.

26
00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.472
Draw magenta arrows on both
receiving magnets, each pointing

27
00:01:14.472 --> 00:01:15.777
towards the other magnet.

28
00:01:15.777 --> 00:01:19.014
The arrows appear before contact
and remain through the spoken

29
00:01:19.014 --> 00:01:19.640
explanation.

30
00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:23.061
Reset the same magnets and
turn one around so north faces

31
00:01:23.061 --> 00:01:23.421
north.

32
00:01:23.421 --> 00:01:24.982
Release from the same gap.

33
00:01:24.982 --> 00:01:25.942
They move apart.

34
00:01:25.942 --> 00:01:29.663
Repulsion means the magnetic
forces push the magnets away from

35
00:01:29.663 --> 00:01:30.324
each other.

36
00:01:30.324 --> 00:01:33.805
The matched camera view makes
the changed pole arrangement

37
00:01:33.805 --> 00:01:34.165
clear.

38
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:51.985
Keep
the magnets, pole arrangement, track and release method

39
00:01:51.985 --> 00:01:52.518
the same.

40
00:01:52.518 --> 00:01:55.537
Change only the starting
gap: one, four, then eight

41
00:01:55.537 --> 00:01:56.248
centimetres.

42
00:01:56.248 --> 00:01:59.859
Measure how far the moving magnet
travels in two seconds, and

43
00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:01.576
repeat every gap three times.

44
00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:04.741
At one centimetre the
magnet moves 14 centimetres.

45
00:02:04.741 --> 00:02:06.703
At four centimetres it moves 6.

46
00:02:06.703 --> 00:02:10.438
At eight centimetres it moves
less than 1 in the same time.

47
00:02:10.438 --> 00:02:13.983
Greater distance gives a
smaller observed effect in this

48
00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:14.363
setup.

49
00:02:14.363 --> 00:02:18.093
A cabinet catch must pull the
door closed before its magnet

50
00:02:18.093 --> 00:02:19.610
touches the steel plate.

51
00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.276
A wide gap gives too little
visible movement; a narrow gap

52
00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:24.224
works reliably.

53
00:02:24.224 --> 00:02:28.143
The design uses attraction across
a gap and distance evidence,

54
00:02:28.143 --> 00:02:29.281
not a hidden hook.

55
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.031
Someone claims magnets
attract every metal.

56
00:02:46.031 --> 00:02:48.628
Test that claim against
the labelled samples.

57
00:02:48.628 --> 00:02:51.975
Steel moves, while aluminium
and copper do not move in the

58
00:02:51.975 --> 00:02:52.783
repeated test.

59
00:02:52.783 --> 00:02:56.245
One clear counterexample is
enough to reject the word every.

60
00:02:56.245 --> 00:02:59.500
Replace the claim: magnets
attract some materials, including

61
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:00.422
the tested steel.

62
00:03:00.422 --> 00:03:03.515
Two magnets may attract or
repel depending on which poles

63
00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:03.786
face.

64
00:03:03.786 --> 00:03:06.336
The observed effect also
changes with distance.

65
00:03:06.336 --> 00:03:09.645
Keep each conclusion tied to
the evidence actually collected.

66
00:03:09.645 --> 00:03:13.111
Three setups wait at equal
gaps: north facing south, north

67
00:03:13.111 --> 00:03:15.562
facing north, and
north facing aluminium.

68
00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:19.088
Predict move together, move
apart or no visible movement in

69
00:03:19.088 --> 00:03:19.686
this test.

70
00:03:19.686 --> 00:03:23.033
For each choice, name the
pole or material evidence that

71
00:03:23.033 --> 00:03:23.750
supports it.
